  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1100 AM MDT Sat Aug 29 2026\n\nUpper high fixed over New Mexico today will continue the week- \nlong trend of above normal temperatures. Lowland highs will again \nflirt with the triple-digit mark, generally in the 96-102 range. \nThis is 5-8 degrees warmer than usual for the end of August. Not \nquite record setting, but still solidly above normal.\n\nDrier trend today compared to the past couple days. Upper high has\nflattened and become more elongated across the south-central US,\ncutting off moisture feed from the southeast. In response,\nprecipitable water values have fallen to 0.9-1.0\" with surface\ndewpoints in the mid-to-upper 40s. This slightly drier setup will\ngreatly reduce storm chances later this afternoon. Isolated\n(10-30% coverage) showers will be limited mostly to higher\nelevations and western New Mexico this afternoon. A few gusty\noutflow winds may be seen, but the vast majority of the forecast\narea will be dry today.\n\nUpper high flattens and begins a progression eastward over the \nweekend. This will focus better moisture further west across \neastern Arizona and western New Mexico, which will be the best \nchances for storms Sunday/Monday. Isolated storms along and west \nthe Continental Divide (Grant and Hidalgo Counties for our \nforecast area) will be capable of gusty winds and blowing dust. \nAdded a 4-8 PM Sunday mention of dust for Lordsburg and the I-10 \ncorridor near the State Line due to sensitivity. Most of the Rio \nGrande/Tularosa basins look to be dry and warm.\n\nWeather pattern looks favorable that at least *marginal* \nprecipitation chances will continue into early September. Upper \nhigh over the Southeastern U.S. and weak southerly flow aloft over\nour region would allow for moisture flow. ECMWF runs have been \nless bullish on moisture, keeping an elongated upper high fixed \nover most of the Southern US. NBM leans hard on ECMWF long term, \nso the extended forecast will have very low PoPs (5-10%). I do \nthink those chances will increase as we head into next week. \nExpecting more isolated (hit-and-miss, mostly miss) showers and \nthunderstorms chances next week, instead of the dry forecast \ncurrently in place.",
